 \subsection{Experimental Setup and Evaluation Protocol}
 \label{sec:experimental_setup}
-Navigation performance, execution-error calibration, margin contributions and
-sensitivity, budget selection, robustness to execution disturbances, and physical
-deployment are evaluated. High-level decisions made through the velocity interface are
-assessed in the planar navigation benchmark, while quadruped execution is examined
-in the hardware study.
-
-The navigation benchmark comprises moderately cluttered, narrow-passage, and
-densely obstructed maps, each spanning $8\times8$ m. Test maps, navigation
-observations, command limits, and the frozen velocity tracker are shared across
-methods, with matched training interaction budgets and network widths. The
-high-level decision interval is 0.2 s, and the episode horizon is 30 s. Each method
-is evaluated using three policies trained with different random seeds. With
-100 trials per policy and scenario, 300 trials are obtained per scenario and
-900 overall. Separate rollout sets are used for calibration, training, budget
-selection, and final evaluation.
-
-Five metrics are reported: success rate (SR), mean executed cost (Cost), physical
-collision rate (CR), budget violation rate (BVR), and mean per-trial minimum
-clearance (MC). Success requires collision-free goal reaching before timeout;
-budget compliance is assessed separately. Collision and clearance are measured
-against a common physical robot footprint. Cost and MC are computed over all
-trials up to goal reaching, collision, or timeout; evaluation continues after
-the budget is exceeded. The reference budgets
-$z_{\rm ref}$ are 120, 140, and 190 a.u. for the moderately cluttered,
-narrow-passage, and densely obstructed scenarios, respectively. The normalized
-initial budget $z_0/z_{\rm ref}$ expresses the initial budget relative to the
-corresponding reference value. Equally weighted means across scenarios are
-reported in the tables at $z_0/z_{\rm ref}=1$. Scenario-level intervals describe
-test-case variability conditional on the trained policies.
-
-The baselines comprise uncalibrated RC-PPO, reach-avoid learning (RA), reward-based
-proximal policy optimization (PPO), and a constrained PPO implementation (CPPO).
-The same frozen velocity tracker is used for all methods. For uncalibrated RC-PPO,
-both execution margins are set to zero. RA retains goal reaching and obstacle
-avoidance but omits cost minimization and the budget input. For PPO and CPPO,
-the collision penalty is weighted by $\beta_L=1$ or $\beta_H=5$, representing
-lower and higher penalty weights, respectively. CPPO additionally imposes the
-expected cumulative-cost constraint $\mathbb{E}[J/z_{\rm ref}]\leq1$.
