 \section{Conclusion}
-The deployment of minimum-cost reach-avoid navigation on quadrupedal robots is
-addressed through hierarchical control and execution calibration in CaMRA. The
-difficulty of jointly learning locomotion and navigation is mitigated by assigning
-motion stabilization to a frozen low-level controller and navigation decisions to
-a high-level policy. Remaining discrepancies between nominal and executed behavior
-are accounted for through separate cost and geometric margins. The reported
-evaluations illustrate the effects of these corrections on budget compliance,
-obstacle clearance, and goal reachability.
-Building on the current geometric navigation formulation, future work will be
-directed toward semantic task understanding and a stochastic formulation in which
-execution uncertainty can be incorporated into formal reach-avoid and cost
-guarantees.
+CaMRA connects quadruped MCRA navigation to execution through hierarchical control
+and calibration. A frozen low-level controller stabilizes motion while a
+high-level policy learns navigation, mitigating coupled learning. Separate cost
+and geometric margins account for residual nominal/execution mismatch. The
+evaluations illustrate their effects on budget compliance, obstacle clearance,
+and goal reachability. Future work will explore semantic task understanding and
+stochastic formulations in which execution uncertainty can be incorporated into
+formal reach-avoid and cost guarantees.
 
